## Issue 2: MEDIUM - Transcript Parsing Reads Entire File

**Problem:** `transform.rs:147-155` and `220-228` read all lines into memory, then slice to last 30/200. O(n) per event.

**Analysis:**
- Bash uses `tail -30` which seeks from end
- Rust reads all lines then takes last N
- For long sessions with thousands of transcript lines, this adds latency

**Implementation:** Use seek-from-end approach with `BufReader` and `Seek`:

```rust
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader, Seek, SeekFrom};

fn read_last_n_lines(path: &str, n: usize) -> Vec<String> {
    let file = match File::open(path) {
        Ok(f) => f,
        Err(_) => return Vec::new(),
    };

    let metadata = match file.metadata() {
        Ok(m) => m,
        Err(_) => return Vec::new(),
    };

    let file_size = metadata.len();
    if file_size == 0 {
        return Vec::new();
    }

    let mut reader = BufReader::new(file);

    // Binary search for start position of last N lines
    // Start from ~4KB from end and expand if needed
    let mut chunk_size = 4096;
    let mut lines = Vec::new();

    loop {
        let start_pos = file_size.saturating_sub(chunk_size);
        if reader.seek(SeekFrom::Start(start_pos)).is_err() {
            break;
        }

        // Skip partial first line (unless at file start)
        if start_pos > 0 {
            let mut partial = String::new();
            let _ = reader.read_line(&mut partial);
        }

        lines.clear();
        for line in reader.by_ref().lines() {
            if let Ok(l) = line {
                lines.push(l);
            }
        }

        if lines.len() >= n || chunk_size >= file_size {
            break;
        }
        chunk_size *= 2;
    }

    // Return last N lines
    if lines.len() > n {
        lines.split_off(lines.len() - n)
    } else {
        lines
    }
}
```

**Files:** `hooks/rust/src/transform.rs`

